  "text": "### Where things live\n\nApp\napp.relevestaffing.com\n\nSite\nrelevestaffing.com\n\nCode\n~/Releve/releve-app\n\nSite files\n~/Releve/releve-site-deploy\n\nBuilt on\nNext.js 15 · Supabase · Netlify\n\nEmail\nGoogle Workspace SMTP, hello@relevestaffing.com\n\nGoogle Cloud\nproject releve-507223\n\n### The two commands\n\nDatabase, then deploy. Both from your own Terminal — the sandboxes Claude\nworks in cannot reach a raw Postgres connection or an authenticated Netlify\nCLI, and there is no repository connected, so no Trigger Deploy button exists.\n\nPGPASSWORD='<database password>' psql -h aws-0-us-east-2.pooler.supabase.com -p 5432 -U postgres.lsionxcnatbozrzmftjh -d postgres -f ~/Releve/releve-app/supabase/schema.sql\n\nRun the whole file, every time; it is written to be re-run. Two NOTICE lines are normal; any ERROR line is not. (psql came with brew install libpq on 11 September.)\n\ncd ~/Releve/releve-app && npx netlify deploy --build --prod\n\nRoll back with\ngit log --oneline then git revert <id>.\n\nIf Google Cloud says your organization does not exist, it is lying.\nOpen shell.cloud.google.com and run gcloud organizations list before\nbelieving the screen. This cost hours once.\n\nNever paste supabase/schema-remaining.sql. It is a\npartial extract (PARTs 25–27 only), and its talent_directory\nis missing security_invoker — the exact bug already fixed twice\nand logged below on 10 September. Running it would replace the safe live view with\nan owner-run one that ignores row-level security, exposing every talent's name,\nphoto, location, psychometric scores, validity flags and Drive facets to anyone\nholding the public anon key. Always run the whole of schema.sql\ninstead — it is written to be re-run — and delete the extract so it\ncannot be reached for by mistake. Verify after any migration with\nselect viewname, definition from pg_views where viewname = 'talent_directory';\nand confirm security_invoker is present.\n\nIf the Supabase SQL editor throws relation \"X\" does not exist\non SQL that is plainly clean — X being an ordinary word from inside a\nstring literal — it is the editor, not the file. Skip the web editor and connect\nstraight to the database instead: Supabase dashboard → Connect → Session\npooler tab (the Direct Connection tab's host is IPv6-only and will not\nresolve from most sandboxes) for the host, port, database and user, then run the\nfile with any Postgres client — psql -f schema.sql, or a short Node\nscript using the pg package's client.query() on the whole\nfile text. Confirmed 10 September: the identical SQL that failed repeatedly in the\nbrowser ran clean on the first try over a direct connection."
